\p
\v 1 Meanwhile Saul¦88438 was still¦88440 spouting off murderous¦88444 threats¦88442 towards¦88445 the followers¦88447 of the \nd master¦88449\nd*. He went to the chief¦88452 priest¦88452
\v 2 and obtained \add official\add* letters¦88457 to enable him¦88456 to enter the \add ≈Jewish¦88463 meeting¦88463 halls¦88463\add* \add (Grk: synagogues)\add* in Damascus¦88459 so that if he found¦88468 either male or female followers of Yeshua, he could¦88468 arrest them and bring¦88478 them to Yerushalem¦88480 in chains.
\p
\v 3 On his journey as he approached Damascus¦88489, suddenly¦88491 light¦88496 from the sky¦88501 flashed¦88495 around¦88495 him.
\v 4 He dropped to his knees\f + \fr 9:4 \ft It seems most likely that Paul and company were walking to Damascus (and not on horseback despite paintings by Caravaggio and others). The journey of somewhere around 300km would have taken around two weeks.\f* and heard¦88507 a voice¦88508 calling, “Saul, Saul, why are you¦88515 persecuting¦88515 me?”
\p
\v 5 “Who are you, master¦88521?” he¦88516 asked.
\p And the voice replied, “I'm \nd Yeshua\nd*, the one that you're¦88532 persecuting¦88533.
\v 6 Now, stand up and go into the city¦88561, and you'll be told there what you need to do¦88569.”
\p
\v 7 Meanwhile his travelling¦88574 companions had¦88576 stood¦88576 there speechless¦88577, because they'd heard¦88578 the voice¦88581 but¦88571 couldn't see anyone¦88579.
\v 8 They helped Saul¦88589 up from the ground¦88592, but¦88587 even when he opened¦88593 his¦88598 eyes¦88597, he couldn't see¦88601 anything. So¦88587 they¦88605 had¦88593 to lead¦88602 him into Damascus¦88607 by hand¦88602.
\v 9 Saul stayed there for three¦88611 days¦88610, unable to see¦88613 and¦88614 not eating¦88616 or drinking anything.
\s1 Ananias restores Saul's sight
\p
\v 10 Now there was a believer there in Damascus¦88626 named Ananias, and¦88622 the master spoke to him¦88632 in a vision¦88636, calling, “Ananias.”
\p “I'm here, master,” he answered.
