\p
\v 11 From Troas¦94919 we¦94920 sailed directly to Samothrace¦94922, and¦94925 then on to Neapolis the following¦94927 day¦94924.
\v 12 From there¦94933 we continued on to Philippi¦94937 which¦94938 is a \add Roman\add* colony¦94948 and¦94951 the capital city of the Macedonia¦94946 district¦94944 where we stayed¦94957 for several days¦94958.
\v 13 On the Rest Days, we went¦94966 \add down\add* to the river¦94973 outside¦94967 the \add city\add* gate¦94969 where¦94974 we thought¦94978 people might be praying, and¦94962 we spoke¦94984 to the women¦94989 who had met there.
\v 14 One of them was Lydia from Thyatira¦94998 who was a seller¦94995 of purple goods\f + \fr 16:14 \ft It's possible that Lydia dyed fabric and sold it, or that she actually made and sold the purple dye itself.\f* and although she already worshipped¦94999 \nd God¦95002\nd*, the \nd master¦95007\nd* opened¦95008 her¦95009 mind¦95010 and she readily accepted what Paul¦95016 was saying.
\v 15 So then Lydia and her¦95024 household were all \add ceremonially\add* immersed¦95019, and then she requested, “If you¦95028 all¦95028 consider me to be faithful¦95030 to the \nd master¦95033\nd*, come¦95035 and stay at my¦95041 place.” So she persuaded us.
\s1 Paul and Silas get thrown into prison
\rem /s1 In Prison at Philippi; Paul and Silas in Prison
\p
\v 16 One time as we were walking to the prayer¦95054 meeting, we met a female slave who was possessed by a spirit¦95059 that enabled her to tell fortunes¦95076, and¦95049 thanks to that, to earn a lot of money for her masters¦95072.
\v 17 She walked along behind us and shouted out, “These¦95089 men¦95091 are slaves¦95092 of the highest¦95096 \nd god¦95094\nd* and they will be telling you¦95101 all¦95101 how to be saved.”
\v 18 After she'd done¦95107 this for several days¦95110, Paul¦95115 was so frustrated that¦95105 he turned¦95117 around and¦95106 spoke to the spirit¦95119 \add in her\add*, “I command¦95123 you¦95124 by the authority of \nd Yeshua\nd* the \nd messiah¦95129\nd* to leave her.”
\p
\v 19 But¦95170 when her¦95152 masters¦95151 realised that their¦95162 profitable business¦95161 was about to come to an end, they¦95173 grabbed¦95167 Paul¦95169 and Silas¦95172 and dragged¦95173 them to the authorities at the marketplace¦95176.
\v 20 They brought¦95181 them forward¦95181 to face the magistrates and¦95180 said¦95186, “These¦95187 Jews are disturbing¦95190 \add the peace\add* in our¦95192 city¦95194
\v 21 and¦95197 teaching behaviour that we as Romans can neither accept¦95207 nor practice¦95211.”
\v 22 An \add angry\add* crowd¦95219 gathered around and the magistrates ordered for Paul and Silas to have¦95228 their¦95229 robes¦95231 torn¦95228 off and then¦95215 to be beaten¦95233 with rods¦95233.
\v 23 They were severely beaten and¦95236 then¦95236 thrown¦95240 into prison where the officer was commanded¦95243 to keep¦95249 them secure¦95248.
\v 24 Because of that, he threw¦95260 them into the inner¦95264 cell and¦95266 locked their¦95267 feet¦95268 in stocks¦95277.
\p
\v 25 Around midnight¦95282 Paul¦95287 and Silas¦95290 were praying¦95291 and singing¦95292 praises to \nd God¦95294\nd*, and the other prisoners¦95301 were listening¦95295 to them.
\v 26 Suddenly¦95302 there was a strong earthquake¦95304 that shook¦95308 even the foundations¦95310 of the prison, and immediately¦95316 all the prison doors¦95318 opened¦95313 up and the chains of all the prisoners came loose.
\v 27 The prison officer woke up, and¦95328 when he saw¦95333 that all the prison doors¦95336 were open¦95334, he drew¦95341 his sword¦95343 to take his own life as he figured that all the prisoners¦95351 must have¦95349 escaped¦95349.
\v 28 But Paul¦95359 shouted out, “Don't harm¦95365 yourself¦95363! We're all still here!”
\p
\v 29 The officer called for torches, then¦95372 rushed¦95375 in and knelt down trembling¦95377 in front of Paul¦95385 and Silas¦95388.
\v 30 Then¦95389 he escorted them out and¦95389 asked, “Masters¦95402, what do I need to do to be saved¦95408?”
\p
\v 31 “Believe¦95412 in the \nd master¦95416 Yeshua¦95417\nd*,” they¦95409 replied, “then¦95410 you and¦95410 your¦95425 household¦95424 can all be saved¦95420.”
\v 32 Then¦95426 they¦95427 explained the message¦95430 about the \nd master¦95433\nd* to him and¦95426 all those in his¦95441 household¦95440.
\v 33 Even though it was still the middle of the night¦95451, he sponged their¦95455 wounds¦95456, and then¦95442 him¦95467 and his¦95467 household were all immersed¦95459 straight away.
\v 34 He took Paul and Silas up into his house¦95479 and gave them a meal, and his whole¦95490 household¦95490 were happy because they now believed¦95492 in \nd God¦95496\nd*.
\p
\v 35 The next day¦95498 the magistrates sent¦95502 officials¦95505 to tell¦95523 the jailer to release Paul and¦95499 Silas.
\p
\v 36 So¦95534 the officer¦95551 told Paul¦95545, “The magistrates \add sent these officials\add* to order¦95552 that you¦95553 two be released.”
\p
\v 37 But¦95581 Paul¦95564 responded, “You mean that after publicly¦95571 beating¦95569 us Roman men¦95573 without a trial, they threw us into prison¦95580 and¦95581 now they want to just quietly release us.”
\p
\v 38 So¦95595 the officials¦95600 reported¦95593 this back to the magistrates, and they were quite afraid¦95614 when they heard¦95617 that Paul and Silas were Roman \add citizens\add*.
\v 39 So¦95622 now they came¦95623 to the prison in person, and appealed¦95631 to them, and escorting them out of prison themselves, asked¦95645 them to please leave the city¦95654.
\v 40 So¦95665 after Paul and Silas left the prison¦95669, they went¦95671 to see¦95677 Lydia¦95675 and after encouraging the believers¦95680 there, they left.
\c 17
\s1 Jason gets in trouble in Thessalonica
\rem /s1 Paul Preaches in Thessalonica; The Uproar in Thessalonica
\p
\v 1 After travelling¦95691 through¦95691 Amphipolis¦95695 and Apollonia¦95698, Paul and Silas went¦95699 to Thessalonica¦95706 where¦95707 there was a \add ≈Jewish¦95710 meeting¦95710 hall¦95710\add*.
\v 2 Paul's custom was to attend at the local meeting hall on the Rest¦95727 Day, so he did that, and¦95715 for three weekends in a row he discussed¦95729 the scriptures¦95736 with them,
\v 3 showing them how that it¦95743 \add had been written\add* that the messiah would suffer¦95744 and be killed and come back to life, and then explaining, “This \nd Yeshua\nd* that I've been telling you¦95761 about is the messiah.”
\v 4 Some of them were persuaded¦95766 and joined¦95768 with Paul¦95770 and Silas¦95773, along with a big group of godly Greeks¦95781 and quite a few prominent women¦95786.
\p
\v 5 But¦95800 some of the other Jews were envious \add of them\add*, and enlisted some of the troublemakers from the marketplaces¦95806 to form¦95814 a mob¦95814. They stirred up all the people, and looking¦95824 for¦95824 Paul and Silas so they could bring¦95827 them out, arrived at the house¦95822 of a man named Jason.
\v 6 But¦95834 when they realised that Paul and Silas weren't there, they dragged¦95836 Jason and some other believers¦95844 off to the city¦95847 leaders, shouting¦95848, “Those two men who've gone around upsetting¦95856 the whole world¦95855 have now come¦95861 here as well
\v 7 and Jason has¦95863 welcomed¦95863 them \add into his house\add*. They rebel against Caesar's decrees¦95870 and claim that \nd Yeshua\nd* is a rival king¦95873.”
\v 8 They stirred up the crowd¦95885 and¦95887 the city¦95891 leaders with these¦95894 accusations,
\v 9 so the leaders collected a heavy fine from Jason¦95901 and the others, and then¦95895 released them.
\s1 Many new believers in Berea
\rem /s1 Paul and Silas in Berea; Paul and Silas in Beroea
\p
\v 10 So¦95908 the believers¦95909 immediately¦95910 sent¦95915 Paul¦95918 and¦95919 Silas¦95921 off during the night¦95914 to go¦95933 to Berea. In Berea they also attended at the \add ≈Jewish¦95929 meeting¦95929 hall¦95929\add*
\v 11 and this group were more upper-class than the congregation in Thessalonica¦95942. They eagerly accepted the message¦95946 and examined¦95953 the scriptures¦95955 every¦95951 day¦95952 to check if what was being taught matched what was written there.
\v 12 Because it did, many of the people believed¦95969 including¦95973 quite a few of the prominent¦95981 Greeks.
\v 13 But¦96023 when the Jews from Thessalonica¦95997 found out that God's message¦96009 was also being proclaimed¦96004 by Paul¦96007 there¦96021 in Berea¦96003, they¦96018 soon turned up to agitate¦96022 and¦96023 stir the crowds¦96026.
\v 14 So¦96030 the believers¦96038 immediately¦96029 sent¦96036 Paul¦96035 off to the coast, but¦96030 Silas¦96054 and¦96030 Timothy¦96057 both stayed.
\v 15 The ones guiding Paul¦96063 took him to Athens¦96067 before they headed back with his instructions for Silas¦96087 and Timothy¦96090 to come¦96097 and join him again as soon as possible.
\s1 People of Athens like new ideas
\rem /s1 Paul in Athens; Paul Preaches in Athens
\p
\v 16 While Paul¦96110 stayed in Athens¦96104 waiting for them, he was upset in his¦96114 spirit¦96113 as he observed¦96118 how idolatrous¦96119 the people were.
\v 17 So¦96125 while he stayed busy having discussions¦96123 with the Jews and other worshippers¦96133 from the meeting¦96128 hall¦96128, he also spent time every¦96140 day¦96141 with anyone¦96124 who just happened¦96144 to be around in the marketplace¦96138.
\v 18 Some of the Epicurean and Stoic philosophers¦96153 enjoyed discussing things with him, but¦96170 others just mocked him, “What does this babbler¦96165 want¦96162 to talk about now?”
\p But¦96150 others said¦96159, “Seems that¦96166 he's talking¦96159 about strange¦96171 demons¦96172,” because¦96176 he was preaching¦96183 the good¦96183 message¦96183 about \nd Yeshua\nd* and coming back to life.
\v 19 So they¦96199 grabbed¦96185 him and brought¦96199 him to the Aries¦96197 Hill \add where the council sat\add* and asked, “Are we¦96204 allowed to know¦96205 what this new¦96208 teaching¦96216 that¦96210 you're bringing is about?
\v 20 It seems that you¦96221 have some surprising¦96217 ideas for our¦96228 ears¦96227, so we¦96229 want¦96235 to hear what it's all about.”
\v 21 (Everyone¦96249 in Athens and¦96242 even visitors¦96244 often took the opportunity¦96251 either to explain or to listen to any new things.)
\p
\v 22 So¦96261 Paul¦96263 stood¦96260 up in the middle of the gathering on the hill and¦96261 spoke, “Men of Athens, I've noticed how you¦96277 all¦96277 are very religious,
\v 23 because¦96280 as I was looking around and¦96281 observing¦96283 your¦96287 objects¦96286 of worship, I even discovered an altar¦96290 that was inscribed¦96295, ‘To the unknown¦96296 \nd god¦96297\nd*.’ So¦96300 without knowing¦96301 it, you already worship this \nd God¦96297\nd* that I'm going to tell you about.
\v 24 This \nd God¦96310\nd* who created the earth and everything in it¦96319 is \nd Yahweh¦96326\nd* who's over heaven¦96321 and earth. He doesn't live in man-made temples¦96330
\v 25 nor can he be repaired by any human¦96338 hands¦96337 and he doesn't need to be fed. \add On the contrary\add*, he's the one who gives life¦96352 and breath¦96354 and who gives us everything that we need.
\v 26 From one man he made¦96359 every¦96364 nationality¦96365 that lives across all the earth¦96376 and designated¦96377 their boundaries¦96384 and their futures.
\v 27 He made them so that they would try to discover \nd God¦96393\nd*, and¦96404 if they did search for him they might find¦96405 him as he's not far from any one of us
\v 28 because¦96423
\q1 ‘We live¦96424 in him and are moved¦96426 by him and exist through him.’
\m Even some of your own poets¦96441 wrote,
\q1 ‘We are all descended¦96447 from just one.’
\m
\v 29 So¦96450 since we are God's offspring, we shouldn't¦96454 be putting gold¦96458 or silver¦96462 or stone¦96464 craftsmanship¦96466 in the same class as the divine¦96472.
\v 30 In fact \nd God¦96485\nd* overlooked¦96483 times¦96478 of such ignorance¦96480 in the past, but now he's informing everyone¦96496 and requiring them to repent¦96497
\v 31 because he's set¦96500 a date for when he's going¦96505 to be fairly judging¦96506 the world¦96509. He's already appointed the judge who everyone can have faith¦96517 in because he brought¦96518 him¦96522 back to life from the dead¦96524.
\p
\v 32 When¦96526 they heard¦96525 about a dead¦96528 person coming back to life, some started mocking¦96531 but¦96526 others¦96533 said¦96534, “We'd like to hear¦96535 more about this from you¦96536 another time.”
\v 33 So Paul¦96545 left¦96546 at that stage
\v 34 but¦96551 some people joined¦96553 with him¦96555 and believed¦96556, including¦96565 Dionysius¦96560 who was a council member, a woman¦96566 named Damaris¦96568, and others¦96570 as well.
\c 18
\s1 Paul resides in Corinth for some time
\rem /s1 Paul in Corinth; Paul Meets Priscilla and Aquila in Corinth
\p
\v 1 After¦96575 this, Paul left Athens¦96584 and travelled to Corinth¦96587.
\v 2 There he came¦96598 across a Jew named Aquila¦96593 who had been born in Pontus but¦96588 who had recently¦96597 moved from Italy¦96602 along with his¦96606 wife¦96605 Priscilla¦96604 because¦96607 \add Emperor\add* Claudius¦96611 had ordered all Jews to leave Rome¦96620. So¦96588 Paul approached¦96627 them,
\v 3 and since they worked in the same¦96635 trade¦96635, he ended up staying with them and they worked together in their craft¦96650 of tent-making.
\v 4 On every¦96662 Rest Day, Paul debated in the \add ≈Jewish¦96659 meeting¦96659 hall¦96659\add*, and was persuading¦96673 both Jews and Greeks¦96681.
\p
\v 5 However when Silas¦96692 and¦96693 Timothy¦96695 arrived from Macedonia¦96688, Paul¦96701 was able to devote his full time to the message¦96698—demonstrating to the Jews that \nd Yeshua\nd* is the \add promised\add* \nd messiah¦96707\nd*.
\v 6 But¦96720 when they opposed¦96717 \add the message\add* and made false accusations, he \add publicly\add* brushed \add the dust\add* off his clothes¦96728\f + \fr 18:6 \ft In the culture, this was a demonstration that you're not responsible for their decision(s).\f* and said¦96730, “Your¦96735 future deaths are your own problem. I'm innocent \add of your guilt.\add* From now on, I'll¦96749 going¦96749 to the non-Jews.”
\v 7 So¦96751 Paul left there¦96756, and went¦96757 to stay at the house of a man named Titos Justus who worshipped¦96769 \nd God¦96771\nd* and whose house was next-door to the meeting¦96778 hall¦96778.
\v 8 Krispo¦96779 the meeting¦96783 hall¦96783 leader¦96783 and all his¦96795 household¦96794 decided to follow the \nd master¦96789\nd*, and many of the Corinthians¦96799 believed¦96785 after they heard¦96800 \add the message\add* and were immersed¦96803.
\p
\v 9 Then¦96816 the \nd master¦96818\nd* spoke to Paul¦96827 one night¦96820 in a vision¦96822, “Don't¦96830 be afraid¦96831 and¦96834 stay silent¦96836, but¦96832 keep¦96836 speaking¦96833 out
\v 10 because I am with you and¦96843 no one can mistreat¦96848 you here because there¦96852 are many of my people¦96851 in this city¦96857.”
\v 11 So Paul stayed on for eighteen months¦96869, teaching¦96871 God's message¦96876 to the people there.
\p
\v 12 Then at the time when Gallio¦96879 was the proconsul¦96883 of Achaia¦96886, the Jews united against Paul¦96898 and¦96900 brought a case to the tribunal¦96909 against him¦96905
\v 13 saying¦96912, “This man¦96919 is going against the law¦96916 by persuading¦96918 people¦96921 to be worshipping¦96922 \add his\add* \nd God¦96924\nd*.”
\p
\v 14 But as Paul¦96928 opened¦96929 his mouth¦96931 to speak, Gallio¦96934 said¦96932 to the Jews, “I would have accepted this charge if it¦96942 was a serious wrong¦96944 or evil¦96948 crime¦96947 and would have listened to you¦96956 Jews.
\v 15 However, since it's a question¦96961 about¦96964 a message¦96965 and names¦96967 and your own law¦96969, you¦96972 all¦96972 can see¦96973 to it yourselves¦96974 because I don't¦96979 want to get involved in judging¦96975 things like that¦96970.”
\v 16 So¦96983 he sent them away from the tribunal¦96989,
\v 17 but¦97004 they¦97000 grabbed¦96991 Sosthenes¦96997, the leader¦96999 from the meeting¦96999 hall¦96999, and¦96992 beat him up right there in front of the tribunal¦97003. But¦96992 Gallio¦97009 took¦96991 action to stop them.
\s1 Paul returns to Syrian Antioch
\rem /s1 Paul's Return to Antioch; Paul Returns to Antioch of Syria; Priscilla, Aquila and Apollos
\p
\v 18 Paul¦97014 stayed on for quite a few days¦97018 after that, then¦97013 he farewelled the believers¦97021 and went to Cenchrea¦97038 where he had his hair cut as was the custom for someone making a vow¦97044. Then¦97013 taking Priscilla¦97031 and Aquila¦97033 with him¦97030, he sailed¦97024 towards Syria¦97027.
\v 19 When¦97048 they¦97045 arrived¦97045 at Ephesus¦97050, he left¦97058 the other two there while he went into the \add ≈Jewish¦97067 meeting¦97067 hall¦97067\add* and¦97048 had¦97064 a discussion¦97070 with the Jews there.
\v 20 They asked¦97073 him to remain¦97081 longer, but¦97075 he¦97085 turned them down
\v 21 although as he gave his farewells, he said¦97091, “I'll¦97106 come back here again if \nd God¦97110\nd* wills it,” then he sailed on from Ephesus¦97118.
\p
\v 22 When¦97119 he landed at Caesarea¦97122, he went¦97129 \add to Yerushalem\add* and¦97119 greeted¦97126 the assembly¦97128 there before continuing on to Antioch¦97131.
\v 23 He stayed there for some time¦97134 before travelling through the regions¦97142 of Galatia¦97141 and¦97143 Frygia strengthening¦97147 \add the faith of\add* the believers \add through his teaching\add*.
\s1 Apollos teaches in Ephesus and Corinth
\rem /s1 Ministry of Apollos; Apollos Instructed at Ephesus; Apollos in Ephesus and Corinth
\p
\v 24 Meanwhile a Jewish man¦97162 named Apollos¦97155 (who had been born in \add the city of\add* Alexandria¦97158) arrived¦97164 in Ephesus¦97166. He knew the \add Hebrew\add* scriptures¦97171 well
\v 25 and had¦97175 been taught¦97194 about the \nd master's¦97184\nd* way¦97181. Being a very enthusiastic person, he started teaching¦97194 about \nd Yeshua\nd*, even though he didn't know¦97201 about being¦97186 filled with the spirit¦97188.\x + \xo 18:25: \xt 2:1-4.\x*
\v 26 So¦97207 Apollos started¦97208 speaking¦97209 out boldly¦97209 in the \add ≈Jewish¦97212 meeting¦97212 hall¦97212\add*. Priscilla¦97218 and Aquila¦97221 listened to him, then¦97207 asked to speak with him privately and explained¦97229 God's way¦97231 more accurately.
\v 27 Now Apollos wanted to go to Achaia¦97274, so the believers encouraged¦97276 him and¦97255 sent a letter ahead of him to tell the believers there to welcome¦97282 him, so when he arrived¦97285 there, he was able to contribute¦97288 a lot to those who through¦97296 God's grace¦97298 had also become believers.
\v 28 Apollos was \add also\add* able to thoroughly¦97303 refute¦97303 the arguments of the Jews and used the scriptures¦97310 to show¦97307 that \nd Yeshua\nd* is the \nd messiah¦97313\nd*.
\c 19
\s1 Many filled with the spirit in Ephesus
\rem /s1 Paul's Third Missionary Journey; Paul in Ephesus
\p
\v 1 So¦97320 then¦97320 while Apollos¦97325 was at Corinth¦97329, Paul¦97330 travelled through¦97348 the interior regions before arriving at Ephesus¦97358. He found¦97361 some followers¦97363 there,
\v 2 and¦97377 asked them if they had received¦97374 the holy¦97373 spirit when they had \add first\add* believed¦97375 \add in Yeshua\add*.
\p They replied, “We hadn't even heard¦97390 that there's¦97389 a holy spirit.”
\p
\v 3 “So¦97405 then¦97405 what were you¦97403 immersed¦97403 into?” asked Paul.
\p “With Yohan's immersion¦97411,” they¦97404 answered.
\p
\v 4 Then¦97413 Paul¦97415 said¦97423, “Yohan¦97416 immersed¦97418 people¦97422 \add in water\add* with an immersion¦97419 that went with repentance¦97420 \add from their sins\add*, but¦97413 he told them to believe in the one who would follow him¦97428, that is to believe in \nd Yeshua¦97436\nd*.”
\p
\v 5 When¦97438 the followers heard¦97437 this, they¦97440 were immersed¦97440 \add in water again, this time proclaiming their faith\add* in \nd Yeshua¦97446\nd* the \nd master¦97445\nd*.
\v 6 Then¦97452 Paul¦97458 placed his hands¦97460 on them \add and prayed\add* and the holy¦97467 \nd spirit¦97465\nd* came¦97463 \add down\add* onto them and they¦97471 started speaking in other languages¦97475 and speaking messages from God.
\v 7 There were about¦97483 twelve¦97484 men¦97482 in the group.
\rem /s1 Paul Ministers in Ephesus
\p
\v 8 So¦97486 Paul started going to the \add ≈Jewish¦97491 meeting¦97491 hall¦97491\add* and for the next three¦97498 months¦97497 he boldly¦97495 discussed¦97499 and persuaded¦97501 them about God's kingdom¦97505.
\v 9 But¦97515 as some of them refused to believe and became hardened¦97514 against his teaching and started telling¦97517 everyone¦97535 that the \add Yeshua\add* way¦97519 was evil¦97517, Paul stopped teaching in the meeting hall and taught the followers¦97533 at Turannos' school¦97540 instead.
\v 10 This went on for two¦97553 years¦97552 to the point that¦97548 everyone in Asia¦97564 \add Minor\add*, whether Jews or Greeks¦97579, heard¦97566 the message¦97570 about Yeshua.
\s1 The power of the master's message
\rem /s1 The Sons of Sceva
\p
\v 11 And \nd God¦97589\nd* was doing¦97590 incredible miracles¦97581 through Paul¦97594
\v 12 such that even if handkerchiefs¦97606 or aprons¦97609 that had touched his¦97605 skin¦97604 were placed on top of any person who was sick or troubled with demons, then they would recover and the evil¦97620 spirits¦97618 would leave them.
\v 13 But some of the Jewish exorcists¦97635 going¦97632 around¦97632 attempted¦97625 to use the name¦97645 of Yeshua to drive out evil¦97643 spirits¦97641 by telling them, “I am imploring¦97652 you¦97653 all¦97653 by Yeshua that Paul¦97660 preaches about.”
\v 14 Seven¦97676 sons¦97677 of the Jewish chief¦97674 priest¦97674, Sceva¦97670 were doing¦97682 this.
\p
\v 15 But¦97726 the evil¦97719 spirit¦97717 would answer¦97713 them saying¦97720, “I know¦97725 \nd Yeshua\nd* and¦97726 I recognise¦97729 Paul¦97728, but¦97731 who are you¦97730?”
\p
\v 16 Then¦97734 the man¦97742 who had the evil¦97751 spirit¦97749 would jump on them and beat them up so they'd end up escaping¦97766 out of the house¦97770 naked¦97763 and wounded¦97765.
\v 17 When¦97773 everyone in Ephesus¦97784 heard about this, both Jews and Greeks¦97780 were frightened, and the name¦97797 of \nd Yeshua\nd* the \nd master¦97799\nd* was being¦97795 honoured.
\v 18 Many of the people who believed¦97805 \add in Yeshua\add* came¦97807 and confessed¦97808 \add their sins\add* and wrong practices¦97812.
\v 19 Lots of the people who'd practiced¦97821 magic¦97820 brought¦97822 their¦97824 scrolls¦97825 and¦97815 burnt¦97826 them in front of the others. When¦97815 the values of all of these were added up, it was \add huge\add*—worth around 50,000 silver¦97836 \add coins\add*.
\v 20 So that was how the \nd master's¦97849\nd* message¦97852 had a powerful¦97841 effect and¦97854 won over many people.
\s1 The silversmiths start a ruckus in Ephesus
\rem /s1 The Riot in Ephesus
\p
\v 21 After all of this, Paul¦97865 decided in his \nd spirit¦97869\nd* that he would need to travel through¦97870 Macedonia¦97873 and¦97860 Achaia¦97876 enroute to Yerushalem¦97881, saying¦97882, “After I've been there¦97888 a while, I will also need to go¦97878 to Rome¦97892.”
\v 22 He sent¦97895 two¦97900 of his workers, Timothy¦97907 and¦97896 Eratos, to Macedonia¦97899 while he stayed on a bit longer in Asia¦97918 \add Minor\add*.
\rem /s1 The Riot in Ephesus
\p
\v 23 But¦97921 while he was still there, a major disturbance¦97926 hit the Yeshua followers.
\v 24 A silversmith¦97937 named Demetrius¦97932 made¦97938 \add little\add* silver¦97941 shrines of Artemis¦97943 and this business¦97952 brought¦97945 a tidy profit to the local craftsmen¦97948.
\v 25 Demetrius gathered all the workers¦97963 together¦97955 and told them, “Men, you¦97971 all¦97971 know¦97971 that¦97974 our prosperity¦97978 comes from this business¦97976 of ours.
\v 26 But¦97982 you've seen yourselves and heard¦97986 from others that not only Ephesus¦97993, but¦97982 almost¦97996 our entire¦97983 \add province of\add* Asia¦97999 \add Minor\add* has¦98005 become¦98019 persuaded¦98005 by this Paul¦98001 fellow, and now the crowds¦98009 are saying¦98010 that what we make are not \add real\add* gods¦98015.
\v 27 However it's not only us and our trade¦98030 that is coming¦98035 into disrepute¦98034, but¦98023 the temple¦98044 of the great goddess¦98041 Artemis is considered to be useless. This could result in a loss of power for the great Artemis that all of Asia¦98066 \add Minor\add* and the inhabited¦98069 world¦98069 have been worshipping¦98070.”
\p
\v 28 When¦98073 the gathering heard¦98072 this, they¦98083 became¦98076 very angry and¦98075 yelled out, “Artemis¦98087 of the Ephesians is great!”
\v 29 Then¦98089 the \add whole\add* city¦98094 was filled¦98090 with confusion¦98097 and the people rushed¦98099 to the theatre¦98105 and grabbed Gaius¦98108 and Aristarchus¦98110 from Macedonia¦98111—travelling¦98113 companions¦98113 of Paul's¦98115.
\v 30 Now Paul¦98117 wanted to go there and speak to the crowd, but¦98119 the believers wouldn't let him¦98129 go.
\v 31 Also¦98134 some of the leaders from Asia \add Minor\add* who were friendly¦98141 towards him, sent¦98142 messengers to him to implore¦98145 him not to go to the theatre¦98152.
\v 32 Some of the people gathered there were yelling out something, but¦98166 overall it was just confusion, and¦98166 the majority of the people couldn't even tell you the reason why they¦98174 were there.
\v 33 Some people in the crowd¦98178 selected Alexander¦98182 the Jew and¦98176 sent him¦98185 forward¦98183 to speak, so he motioned with his hand¦98195 ready to explain the situation to the people.
\v 34 But as soon as they recognised¦98202 that he was a Jew, people started yelling, “Artemis¦98222 of the Ephesians is great,” and this continued for over two¦98217 hours¦98216.
\p
\v 35 Eventually the city¦98253 administrator was able to calm the crowd¦98234 and tell¦98237 them, “Men, fellow Ephesians, what person from around here doesn't know¦98249 that it's Ephesus that's the temple¦98254 keeper¦98254 of the great¦98259 Artemis¦98261 and of this image¦98264 that fell down from Zeus?
\v 36 Since all of this is unquestionable¦98265, it¦98271 makes sense for everyone to calm down and¦98275 not do¦98278 anything reckless¦98277.
\v 37 These men¦98282 that you¦98279 presented here haven't stolen anything from the temple¦98287, nor have they slandered¦98290 our¦98294 goddess¦98292.
\v 38 So if Demetrius¦98299 and the silver craftsmen¦98307 with him¦98306 have¦98308 a case against¦98309 anyone¦98311, they should¦98319 bring¦98315 it to the courts¦98314 and the proconsuls¦98317 and register the charges.
\v 39 But for the rest of you, if you have a grievance against others¦98322, it¦98337 needs to be settled¦98337 in a lawful¦98334 assembly¦98336
\v 40 because¦98339 we're in danger¦98340 of being charged with insurrection¦98343 about what happened today¦98346. And we would have¦98365 no defence that¦98352 we could respond with concerning this disorderly¦98361 gathering¦98361.” Then¦98363 he told them all to go home.
\c 20
\s1 Paul travels to Macedonia and Achaia
\rem /s1 To Macedonia and Achaia; Through Macedonia and Greece; Paul Goes to Macedonia and Greece
\p
\v 1 After all the commotion¦98374 had died down, Paul¦98378 sent¦98375 for the believers, and after¦98369 greeting¦98386 and encouraging them, he left¦98388 \add Ephesus\add* to go¦98390 to Macedonia¦98393.\f + \fr 20:1 \ft As he'd already decided—see 19:21.\f*
\v 2 As he travelled through¦98394 the districts¦98398, he challenged the people there with a lot of messages, until he eventually arrived in Greece¦98410.
\v 3 He'd been there about three¦98415 months¦98414 when he heard about a plot¦98420 by some Jews to waylay him¦98421 as he boarded a ship to return to Syria¦98431, so instead he decided to return through¦98442 Macedonia¦98444
\v 4 with Sopater¦98456 son of Pyrrhus¦98457 from Berea, Aristarchus¦98461 and Secundus¦98463 from Thessalonica¦98459, Gaius¦98465 from Derbe, Tychicus¦98474 and Trophimus¦98476 from the province of Asia¦98471 \add Minor\add*, and Timothy¦98469 all accompanying¦98445 him¦98450.
\v 5 They went ahead and¦98478 waited for us\f + \fr 20:5 \ft Luke (the writer) includes himself here.\f* at Troas¦98485.
\v 6 So¦98487 we sailed¦98488 out from Philippi¦98495 after the \it Flat Bread¦98493 Celebration\it*. We arrived and meet the others there at Troas¦98502 after a five-day trip, and then¦98487 stayed¦98513 on there for seven¦98515 days.
\s1 Paul's all-night discussions in Troas
\rem /s1 Paul's Last Visit to Troas; Paul's Final Visit to Troas; Paul's Farewell Visit to Troas; Eutychus Raised From the Dead at Troas
\p
\v 7 On the Sunday we had¦98524 gathered¦98524 together to break¦98529 bread¦98530 together and¦98518 Paul¦98532 was discussing¦98533 many things with them. As he was scheduled to leave the next day, the meeting went¦98535 on towards midnight¦98544
\v 8 and¦98546 there¦98545 were many lamps¦98548 in the upper¦98552 room¦98552 where¦98553 we¦98554 had¦98556 all gathered¦98556.
\v 9 Now there was a young¦98561 man¦98561 named Eutychus¦98563 who was sitting¦98557 on the window¦98567 sill, and as Paul¦98576 went on, he fell¦98584 asleep and toppled down from the third¦98587 storey. The ones who reached him first thought he was dead¦98592,
\v 10 but¦98594 Paul¦98596 had come¦98593 downstairs and bent over Eutychus and held him in his¦98612 arms and said¦98606 to the crowd, “Don't¦98607 worry, he's still alive.”
\v 11 Then¦98617 Paul went¦98633 back up and broke¦98619 the bread¦98621 with the others, eating a little and continuing to talk with them until¦98629 leaving at daybreak¦98630.
\v 12 The locals took the young man home and¦98643 were very relieved that he was alive.
\s1 Sailing from Troas to Miletus
\rem /s1 From Troas to Miletus; The Voyage from Troas to Miletus; Paul Meets the Ephesian Elders; Paul's Farewell to the Ephesian Elders
\p
\v 13 We went ahead to the ship¦98655 and departed for Assos¦98660 where it had been arranged that we'd pick up Paul¦98665 because¦98667 he wanted to walk there¦98661.
\v 14 So¦98678 when we met him¦98688 at Assos¦98686, he boarded the ship and¦98678 we all sailed on to Mitylene¦98691.
\v 15 After we left Mitylene, we were out from Kios the next day, in Samos¦98706 the day after that, and¦98692 then¦98692 the following¦98713 day we arrived at Miletus¦98717.
\v 16 Paul¦98722 had¦98718 decided to sail¦98723 past¦98723 Ephesus¦98725 because¦98739 he was hurrying¦98738 to try to get to Yerushalem¦98753 for the Day¦98747 of Pentecost¦98749 so he didn't¦98727 want to spend¦98732 time¦98732 in Asia¦98737 \add Minor\add*.
\s1 Paul's farewell to the Ephesian elders
\rem /s1 Paul Speaks to the Ephesian Elders; Paul's Farewell Speech to the Elders of Ephesus
\p
\v 17 So¦98756 while we were at Miletus¦98758, he sent¦98759 a message to Ephesus¦98761 for the elders¦98765 of the assembly¦98767 to come to him.
\v 18 When¦98769 they¦98770 arrived¦98770, he told them, “You¦98781 all¦98781 know¦98782 that from the very first¦98785 day¦98786 that I set¦98790 foot¦98790 in Asia¦98793 \add Minor\add* and¦98769 for the entire¦98781 time¦98807 that I was there
\v 19 I humbly served¦98810 the \nd master¦98812\nd* even through tears¦98820 and trials¦98822 when the Jews plotted to destroy me.
\v 20 But¦98842 I didn't¦98839 shrink¦98834 back from teaching you everything that¦98838 would help you, whether I was teaching in public¦98845 or in individual homes¦98848.
\v 21 I explained to both Jews and Greeks¦98855 how they needed to turn their backs on their sins and towards \nd God¦98859\nd* with faith¦98862 in our¦98870 \nd master¦98868 Yeshua\nd* the \nd messiah¦98873\nd*.
\p
\v 22 However the \nd spirit¦98882\nd* is now forcing me towards Yerushalem¦98885 even though I don't¦98894 know¦98896 what will happen to me there.
\v 23 I just know that recently in every¦98904 city¦98906, the holy¦98902 \nd spirit¦98900\nd* tells me that chains and¦98915 hardships are waiting for me.
\v 24 But¦98949 I've never regarded my own life as being valuable—I only want to reach the end of my life having served in the way that¦98954 the \nd master¦98960 Yeshua\nd* instructed me to speak out the good¦98967 message¦98967 about God's grace¦98969.
\p
\v 25 So¦98972 you see, I know¦98976 that all of you from here where I passed¦98990 through¦98990 proclaiming¦98991 God's kingdom¦98993, you will never see me again.
\v 26 That's why I'm declaring to all of you¦99002 today¦99006 that I'm not guilty of your judgement
\v 27 because¦99018 I didn't¦99017 avoid telling you¦99030 anything that¦99020 I had from \nd God¦99028\nd*.
\v 28 So now you elders look after your own lives and¦99035 those of your flock¦99038 because the holy¦99045 \nd spirit¦99043\nd* chose you as overseers¦99048 to shepherd¦99049 the assembly¦99051 of God's people who he paid for with his very own blood¦99062.
\v 29 I'm aware that after¦99072 I leave, vicious wolves¦99077 will come¦99071 in amongst¦99079 you¦99080 and trying to steal the flock¦99084.
\v 30 Even some of you¦99087 will rise¦99089 up and¦99085 teach a message that's warped but¦99085 which will draw¦99095 some of the believers away to follow it,
\v 31 so take great care. Remember¦99103 that for three¦99105 years, I never stopped warning each of you along with my tears¦99113
\v 32 so now I'm leaving you¦99122 all¦99122 in God's hands and¦99118 with the message¦99130 of his¦99133 grace¦99132 which¦99134 is quite capable of helping you grow to receive the inheritance¦99143 that's for all those who've been purified.
\p
\v 33 I never envied anyone's gold¦99155 or silver¦99152 or \add fancy\add* clothes¦99157
\v 34 and you¦99163 yourselves¦99161 know¦99163 that I laboured with my own hands¦99179 to cover my expenses and for those with me.
\v 35 Yes, I showed¦99185 you¦99186 all¦99186 that it's good to work and to take¦99191 care¦99191 of the weaker ones and to remember¦99195 the message¦99198 of the \nd master¦99200 Yeshua\nd* when he said¦99205 that it's better to be a giver than a taker.”
\p
\v 36 When¦99214 he'd finished speaking, Paul knelt¦99217 down and¦99214 prayed¦99224 with them all.
\v 37 Everyone¦99238 was sobbing as they¦99239 hugged and¦99232 kissed him¦99240
\v 38 because what he'd said about never seeing him again had¦99248 really distressed¦99241 them. Then¦99260 they accompanied¦99259 him to his¦99257 ship¦99265.
\c 21
\s1 Paul goes to Yerushalem
\rem /s1 On to Jerusalem; Paul Goes to Jerusalem; Paul's Journey to Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 When¦99268 it¦99269 was time for us to sail, we¦99285 left them and sailed directly across to Cos¦99288 before going on to Rhodes¦99295 the next¦99292 day¦99289 and then¦99268 on to Patara¦99298.
\v 2 From there we found¦99302 a ship¦99303 heading to Phoenicia¦99306, so we boarded¦99307 that and¦99301 set off.
\v 3 After sighting Cyprus¦99313 and then¦99311 leaving it¦99317 behind on our left, we sailed¦99319 on to Syria¦99321 and docked there¦99327 at the port of Tyre¦99326 where the ship¦99331 had cargo¦99335 to be unloaded¦99333.
\v 4 We searched for the believers there¦99343 and¦99338 then¦99338 stayed on with them for seven¦99345 days¦99344. They were inspired by the \nd spirit¦99352\nd* to tell¦99349 Paul¦99348 not to go to Yerushalem¦99359.
\v 5 Once our time¦99367 was up there and everyone knew that we¦99369 were leaving, even the wives¦99374 and children¦99376 accompanied¦99370 us out of the city¦99380 to the port. We all knelt¦99382 on the beach and prayed¦99390
\v 6 before we continued on and boarded our ship¦99402 and they went¦99398 back to their¦99407 own homes¦99408.
\p
\v 7 So¦99410 we sailed from Tyre¦99415 down to Ptolemais¦99419 where we were able to greet¦99421 the brothers¦99423 and sisters¦99423 and stay overnight with them.
\v 8 The next¦99432 day¦99430 we continued on to Caesarea¦99441 and ended up in Philip's house¦99446. He was one of the \add original\add* seven¦99454 \add men who had been given the responsibility of distributing aid\add*—a preacher¦99449 of the good¦99449 message¦99449, and we stayed with him¦99457.
\v 9 Philip had four¦99464 unmarried daughters¦99462 still at home, and¦99459 they shared messages from God.
\v 10 Several days¦99471 later a man named Agabus¦99480 came¦99473 from Yudea¦99477. He too shared messages from God
\v 11 and when he arrived, he picked up Paul's belt and tied¦99493 it around his own feet¦99499 and hands¦99504, telling them that the holy¦99512 \nd spirit¦99510\nd* had told him that this is how the owner of the belt would be treated by the Jewish leaders in Yerushalem¦99524 before they¦99528 would then hand him over to the non-Jews to be dealt with.
\p
\v 12 When¦99536 we heard¦99537 this described, both we and¦99536 the locals¦99545 begged Paul not to go¦99551 to Yerushalem¦99554
\v 13 but he answered¦99556, “Oh come on, what's all this weeping¦99568 and¦99569 carrying on that's breaking¦99571 my¦99572 heart¦99574? Actually I'm quite ready¦99587 not only to be tied¦99579 up, but even to be killed in Yerushalem¦99586 as a testimony to the \nd master¦99595 Yeshua\nd*.”
\p
\v 14 When¦99600 we saw that we couldn't change his mind, we didn't¦99598 say¦99604 anything else, figuring out that whatever the \nd master¦99608\nd* wills for the situation would happen¦99614,
\rem /s1 Paul Arrives at Jerusalem
\v 15 and so after¦99616 these¦99621 several days¦99620, we¦99627 had¦99622 gotten everything ready and started off for Yerushalem¦99631.
\v 16 Some of the believers from Caesarea¦99639 accompanied us and¦99633 led us to the home of a man named Mnason¦99647 to stay—he was an older believer from Cyprus¦99649.
\s1 Paul visits Yacob and makes a vow
\rem /s1 Paul's Arrival at Jerusalem; Paul Visits James at Jerusalem
\p
\v 17 When¦99653 we arrived at Yerushalem¦99656, the brothers¦99662 and¦99653 sisters¦99662 there happily welcomed¦99658 us,
\v 18 and the next day¦99663, we accompanied Paul¦99672 to go and see Yacob\f + \fr 21:18 \ft Yacob is commonly (but wrongly) called James in most older English Bibles.\f* and the elders¦99681 of the assembly there.
\v 19 After greeting¦99685 them, Paul then¦99683 described all the things that \nd God¦99701\nd* had done¦99699 among the non-Jews as he had served among them,
\v 20 and when they'd heard¦99711 all that¦99732 they praised¦99712 \nd God¦99715\nd* and told Paul, “Brother¦99722, you've noticed how many thousands¦99724 of Jews have believed¦99733 \add in the messiah\add* but¦99710 they still strive to observe¦99721 \add Mosheh's\add* laws.
\v 21 But¦99742 they've heard reports that you've been teaching¦99747 the Jews that live outside our country that they don't have to obey what Mosheh¦99749 \add wrote\add*—telling them that they don't have to have their¦99763 children¦99764 circumcised¦99761 and¦99742 that they don't have to follow our \add Jewish\add* customs¦99769.
\v 22 What will happen \add now\add*, because \add the leaders here\add* will certainly hear¦99781 that you¦99784 have¦99784 arrived?
\v 23 So¦99786 here's what we¦99790 suggest that¦99785 you¦99789 do¦99787: Four of our men are making a \add public\add* vow¦99795.
\v 24 Why don't you join these¦99800 four and join them in the purification¦99802 ceremony \add at the temple\add* and pay their expenses in getting your heads shaved. Then everyone will be able to see that¦99824 it's false what they've heard about¦99826 you, because you \add clearly\add* still comply with \add Mosheh's\add* instructions¦99825.
\v 25 And as for the believers¦99845 who are non-Jews, we've informed them of our recommendation that they should avoid anything that's been offered to an idol¦99870, avoid consuming blood¦99873 and meat from strangled¦99875 animals¦99875 and birds, and avoid sexual¦99877 immorality¦99877.”
\rem /s1 Paul Is Arrested
\p
\v 26 So the next day¦99887 Paul¦99880 took¦99881 the \add four\add* men¦99883 for the purification¦99890 ceremony in the temple¦99895 and there they announced¦99896 the date when their vows would be fulfilled¦99898 and their sacrifices made.
\s1 Paul is captured inside the temple
\rem /s1 Paul Is Arrested in the Temple; Paul's work in Jerusalem
\p
\v 27 When¦99915 the seven¦99919 days¦99921 \add of the vow\add* were almost over, some Jews from \add the province of\add* Asia¦99929 \add Minor\add* noticed Paul in the temple¦99936. They stirred up the crowd¦99946 and¦99915 grabbed hold of him,
\v 28 yelling out, “Men of Yisrael. Help¦99959 us here. This is the man¦99963 who's been teaching¦99981 all around the world against Yisrael and against \add Mosheh's\add* law¦99970 and against this temple. What's more he brought¦99986 non-Jews here into the temple and so has¦99993 defiled¦99993 this holy¦99995 place¦99996.”
\v 29 (\add They said this\add* because¦99999 they had previously¦100001 seen¦100001 Trofimus¦100002 from Ephesus in the city¦100007 with Paul¦100019 and had assumed that he had brought¦100017 him¦100009 into the temple¦100016.)
\p
\v 30 So¦100021 now the whole¦100024 city¦100023 was in an uproar, and the crowds rushed in, grabbed¦100031 Paul¦100033, and dragged¦100034 him¦100035 out of the temple and hurriedly closed the temple gates.
\v 31 They were ready to kill¦100051 Paul, but¦100048 the commander¦100055 of the Roman soldiers had heard that all Yerushalem¦100062 was in an uproar.
\v 32 Taking some soldiers and officers, he immediately¦100064 ran¦100070 down to the temple, and when the crowd saw¦100075 the commander¦100077 and the soldiers they¦100082 stopped beating Paul¦100085.
\v 33 The commander¦100090 came close and took¦100091 hold¦100091 of Paul and ordered¦100094 that he be tied¦100095 up with a pair of chains¦100096 while he tried to find out who he was and what he'd done¦100107 wrong.
\v 34 Various people in the crowd¦100117 were shouting out different things, and¦100122 not being able¦100121 to make any sense of it because¦100127 of all the commotion¦100129, the commander¦100130 ordered¦100130 that Paul be taken into the barracks¦100135.
\v 35 When¦100137 they got to the stairs¦100142, the soldiers¦100150 had to lift him¦100147 up above their heads because¦100151 of the frenzy of the crowd¦100156,
\v 36 because¦100158 most of the people¦100162 were following¦100157 and shouting out, “Do away with him¦100167!”
\s1 Paul's defence
\rem /s1 Paul Speaks to the Crowd; Paul Defends Himself
\p
\v 37 As they were able to carry him into the fortress, Paul¦100175 called down to the commander, “Can I say¦100185 something¦100186 to you¦100188?”
\p “You speak Greek!”, replied the commander.
\v 38 “Aren't you¦100196 the Egyptian who stirred up a group of assassins¦100215 a while back and led¦100207 them out into the wilderness¦100210?”
\p
\v 39 “No, I'm a Jew from Tarsus¦100227 in Cilica—a citizen¦100235 of an important city¦100234,” said¦100216 Paul¦100219. “But please, let me speak¦100243 to these people¦100246.”
\p
\v 40 The commander nodded, so the soldiers stood¦100256 Paul on the stairs¦100259 where he gestured¦100262 to the people¦100267. The crowd quietened down, and¦100249 so Paul spoke loudly in his¦100248 Hebrew¦100280 language¦100281:
\c 22
\nb
\v 1 “Men, brothers¦100284, and¦100285 fathers¦100286, listen¦100287 to what I have to say for myself.”
\v 2 When¦100296 they heard¦100295 him speaking Hebrew¦100299, they became even more quiet¦100305 as he continued,
\v 3 “I am a Jew who grew up in this very city¦100327 to study under Gamaliel¦100332. I've been raised according¦100335 to every part of our¦100337 ancestral¦100338 law¦100339, working hard to obey \nd God¦100343\nd* like all of you¦100347 here today¦100350.
\v 4 I used to persecute¦100356 the followers of ‘The Path’ even to death¦100359—tying up both men¦100366 and women¦100369 and handing them over to the prisons¦100364
\v 5 as the Chief¦100373 Priest¦100373 and all the council¦100383 of elders¦100383 can tell \add you\add*. I took letters¦100387 from them to our \add Jewish\add* brothers¦100393 in Damascus¦100396 when I went so that I could¦100410 arrest the ones there¦100402 and bring¦100398 them back here to Yerushalem¦100407 to be punished¦100410.
\s1 Paul explains about his conversion
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Conversion
\r (Acts 9:1-19; 26:12-18)
\p
\v 6 “But¦100416 one time as I was travelling and¦100416 getting close to Damascus¦100419 by the middle of the day, suddenly¦100424 a bright light¦100432 from the sky¦100428 began to flash¦100429 around me
\v 7 and¦100442 I fell¦100437 to the ground¦100441. I heard¦100443 a voice¦100444 asking me, ‘Saul, Saul, why are you¦100451 persecuting¦100451 me?’
\v 8 ‘Who are you, master¦100459?’ I answered¦100454. And the voice said¦100460, ‘I am \nd Yeshua\nd*—the one from \nd Nazareth¦100470\nd* that you are persecuting¦100473.’
\v 9 The other¦100488 people with me saw¦100482 the light¦100481 but¦100475 didn't¦100490 hear¦100491 the voice¦100489 that spoke¦100493 to me.
\v 10 Then¦100496 I asked, ‘What do I need to do, master?’ ‘Get up and¦100496 continue into Damascus¦100509,’ the master said. ‘When¦100496 you get there¦100510, you'll be told everything that you've been assigned to do.’
\v 11 But I couldn't see anything because¦100526 the brightness of the light¦100530 \add had affected my eyes\add*, so my companions had to lead¦100532 me by hand¦100532 into Damascus¦100540.
\p
\v 12 “In Damascus there was a man¦100544 named Ananias¦100541—a \add non-Jew\add* but¦100542 who was respected by the Jews there because he strove to obey \add Mosheh's\add* instructions about living¦100555.
\v 13 Ananias came¦100557 to where I was and standing¦100561 by me said¦100562, “Brother¦100565 Saul¦100564, receive¦100566 your sight¦100566!” That very hour¦100570 I was able to see again and look¦100572 at him¦100574
\v 14 and he said¦100577, ‘The \nd god¦100579\nd* of our¦100582 ancestors¦100581 has decided in advance to reveal his¦100589 plans to you, and for you to see¦100591 the sinless one—to hear¦100595 him speaking
\v 15 because he wants you to inform people¦100607 everywhere what you've¦100610 seen¦100610 and¦100611 heard¦100612.
\v 16 So¦100613 what else are you waiting¦100616 for? Get up and¦100613 wash¦100620 away your¦100623 sins¦100622 by getting immersed¦100618 \add in water\add* as you request his¦100629 forgiveness.’
\s1 The crowd riots when Paul mentions preaching to non-Jews
\rem /s1 Paul Sent to the Gentiles; Paul's Call to Preach to the Gentiles
\p
\v 17 “Then¦100631 when I returned¦100633 to Yerushalem¦100635 and¦100636 was praying¦100637 in the temple¦100641, I fell into a trance¦100645
\v 18 and saw¦100647 \add @the master\add* telling me, ‘Get out of Yerushalem¦100658 in a hurry because¦100659 they¦100661 won't¦100660 accept¦100661 what you're teaching them about me.’
\v 19 ‘\nd Master¦100669\nd*,’ I replied, ‘they¦100670 know¦100671 that previously I was the one imprisoning¦100675 and¦100676 beating¦100677 the people who believed¦100682 in you¦100684,
\v 20 and when Stephen¦100690 was killed¦100709 after teaching about you¦100693, it was me standing¦100698 there and cheering on the ones executing him¦100710 as I held their coats for them.’
\v 21 Then¦100711 \add @the master\add* told me, ‘Get going¦100715 now because¦100716 I'm sending¦100722 you¦100724 away to the non-Jews.’ ”
\rem /s1 Paul the Roman Citizen; Paul and the Roman Tribune
\p
\v 22 Until this point in his message¦100732, the crowd had been listening to Paul, but¦100727 now they started shouting, “Do away with this man¦100744! He doesn't deserve to live¦100750 in this world!”
\v 23 Then they yelled and threw some of their¦100758 clothes¦100759 down onto the ground and threw dust¦100761 up into the air¦100766.
\rem /s1 Paul Reveals His Roman Citizenship
\v 24 So the commander¦100770 ordered Paul to be brought¦100771 inside into barracks¦100776 and ordered that he be flogged¦100778 and interrogated¦100779 to determine what he'd done that would make the crowd react like that.
\v 25 But¦100815 as they¦100797 strapped¦100802 him down and¦100815 stretched¦100797 him out for the flogging¦100820, Paul¦100809 asked the centurion¦100807 standing¦100806 there, “It is legal for you¦100819 to flog a Roman citizen who hasn't even been tried?”
\p
\v 26 When¦100823 he heard¦100822 that¦100845, the centurion¦100825 went to the commander¦100833 and¦100823 reported¦100835, “How are we going¦100840 to handle this, because¦100843 this man¦100844 is a Roman citizen?”
\p
\v 27 So¦100850 the commander¦100852 went in to where Paul was and¦100850 asked him¦100855, “Tell me the truth. Are you¦100860 a Roman citizen?”
\p “Yes I am,” said¦100854 Paul.
\p
\v 28 “Yeah, and¦100872 I paid a lot for mine as well,” mocked the commander¦100875.
\p “Actually mine is through birth,” Paul¦100890 responded.
\p
\v 29 At this point, the soldiers who were about to interrogate¦100906 Paul left the room, and even the commander¦100909 was now afraid¦100911 after learning¦100912 that he'd had a Roman citizen tied¦100921 down to be flogged.
\s1 Paul faces the Jewish council
\rem /s1 Paul's trials; Paul before the Council; Paul before the High Council; Before the Sanhedrin
\p
\v 30 The next¦100924 day¦100922, the Roman commander¦100942 still wanted to know¦100926 what was behind the anger of the Jews, so he had¦100954 Paul released¦100936 from the cell, but¦100923 brought¦100954 him down to a room where he'd ordered¦100942 the chief¦100946 priests¦100946 and the Jewish council¦100951 to convene. Paul was given a seat
\c 23
\nb
\v 1 and looking¦100960 directly at the council¦100965 members said¦100968, “Men, brothers¦100970, all my life I have¦100975 lived¦100975 before \nd God¦100977\nd* as a good¦100974 citizen¦100975 and with a clean conscience¦100973.”
\v 2 But¦100983 Ananias¦100985 the chief¦100984 priest¦100984 commanded¦100987 one of his¦100992 companions to whack Paul on the mouth¦100994.
\v 3 “\nd God¦101008\nd* will strike¦101004 you,” said¦101003 Paul¦100997, “you grand show-off. You've been brought here to judge¦101014 me as someone who knows the law and¦101011 yet you violate¦101020 the law by commanding¦101021 that I be hit in the face.”
\p
\v 4 But the others standing¦101026 there said¦101027, “Are you¦101032 insulting¦101032 God's chief¦101029 priest¦101029?”
\p
\v 5 “Brothers¦101039,” said¦101033 Paul¦101036, “I didn't¦101037 realise that he's the chief¦101042 priest¦101042, because¦101044 the scriptures teach us not to speak¦101051 wrongly¦101052 of our leaders.”
\p
\v 6 But¦101082 then, knowing¦101053 that the council¦101071 consisted of some from the sect¦101062 of the Sadducees¦101062 and some from the Pharisees' party, Paul¦101056 continued loudly, “Men, brothers¦101073, like my father, I'm a member¦101075 of the Pharisees and I'm being¦101086 tried today concerning¦101080 the hope¦101081 and the resurrection¦101083 of the dead¦101084!”
\p
\v 7 Well, as soon as he said this, the council was split as the Pharisees¦101098 and¦101088 the Sadducees¦101102 started arguing.
\v 8 (Sadducees¦101108 say¦101111 that¦101123 the dead don't come back to life, and there's no messengers¦101117 and no spirits¦101119, but¦101121 the Pharisees¦101120 believe there is.)
\v 9 So¦101126 then¦101126 there was a big kerfuffle because some of the teachers¦101135 of the law spoke up for the Pharisees¦101140 saying¦101144, “We don't see anything wrong with this man¦101150. Maybe it was a spirit¦101154 or one \add of God's\add* messengers¦101158 that¦101151 spoke to him¦101156.”
\p
\v 10 But¦101162 the \add Roman\add* commander¦101172 was afraid¦101169 that they might tear¦101174 Paul apart¦101174 in the big argument, so he called for his soldiers to come¦101184 down to uplift Paul and¦101162 return him to the barracks¦101196.
\p
\v 11 The following¦101199 night¦101200, the \nd master¦101204\nd* appeared to Paul and told him¦101202, “Be brave because¦101209 just like you testified¦101210 about me here in Yerushalem¦101216, so too you'll tell them about me in Rome¦101222.”
\s1 The plan to kill Paul
\rem /s1 The Plan to Kill Paul; The Plot to Kill Paul; The Plot against Paul's Life
\p
\v 12 The next day¦101227, some of the Jews gathered¦101231 informally and¦101226 pledged not to eat¦101244 or drink¦101246 until¦101247 they'd killed¦101251 Paul¦101255.
\v 13 There were more than forty¦101259 of them in this conspiracy¦101265,
\v 14 and they approached¦101269 the chief¦101271 priests¦101271 and elders¦101274 and told them, “We've¦101276 made an oath to taste¦101280 nothing¦101279 until¦101283 we've killed¦101286 Paul¦101288, otherwise we'll bring a curse¦101276 on ourselves¦101278.
\v 15 So¦101290 you guys assemble the council¦101305 and report¦101300 to the commander¦101302 so that¦101332 he'll bring¦101309 him down to you¦101292 all¦101292 as if you need more information about his actions, and then we'll have an opportunity to kill¦101333 before¦101325 he gets here.
\p
\v 16 But¦101351 a nephew of Paul had heard¦101339 about this ambush¦101348 and¦101351 went in to the barracks¦101356 to inform Paul.
\v 17 Paul¦101363 then¦101361 called¦101360 one of the Roman centurions¦101366 and¦101361 told him to take the young¦101370 man¦101370 to the commander¦101376 because¦101378 he had something¦101381 to tell¦101368 him.
\v 18 So¦101385 the centurion took¦101386 him to the commander¦101391, explaining, “The prisoner¦101395 Paul¦101396, called¦101397 me and¦101392 asked¦101399 me to bring¦101388 this young¦101402 man¦101402 to you because he has something¦101408 to report to you.”
\p
\v 19 The commander¦101418 took¦101411 the young man by the hand¦101415 and led him¦101416 into his¦101416 private office and asked, “What do you¦101428 have to tell me?”
\p
\v 20 “The Jews have decided¦101436 to ask¦101438 you,” he said¦101431, “that¦101437 tomorrow¦101441 you bring¦101444 Paul¦101443 down to the council¦101447 as if they wanted to question him¦101460 further.
\v 21 But¦101485 don't¦101463 listen to them because¦101467 over forty¦101473 of their¦101469 men¦101471 will be lying¦101466 in wait¦101466 for him, and¦101485 they've sworn not to eat¦101478 or drink¦101480 until¦101481 they've killed¦101483 him. In fact, they're all set up already, just waiting¦101490 for your consent.”
\p
\v 22 The commander¦101498 told¦101503 the young¦101501 man¦101501 not to tell¦101505 anyone¦101504 else about the ambush and dismissed him.
\s1 Paul is escorted away in the night
\rem /s1 Paul Is Sent to Caesarea; Paul Transferred to Caesarea; Paul Is Sent to Governor Felix; Paul Sent to Felix the Governor
\p
\v 23 Then¦101511 the commander called¦101512 in two of his centurions¦101517 and ordered them to prepare two-hundred soldiers to travel as far as Caesarea¦101525, supported by seventy¦101528 mounted soldiers and two-hundred more with spears¦101530. They were to be ready to leave by 9pm,
\v 24 along with a horse for Paul¦101543 to ride, and¦101538 were to get him safely¦101544 to Governor¦101548 Felix¦101546.
\v 25 Then the commander wrote¦101565 a letter¦101568 ready to take:
\p
\v 26 “Greetings¦101584 to the most excellent¦101581 Governor¦101582 Felix¦101583 from Claudius Lysias¦101578.
\v 27 I'm writing to you about this man¦101586 who had been captured¦101591 by the Jews here and¦101595 they were about to kill¦101597 him when I moved in with my soldiers because he is a Roman citizen.
\v 28 I wanted to know¦101619 what they¦101626 were accusing him of, so I took him down to their¦101633 council¦101632 meeting,
\v 29 but¦101645 I discovered that it was just some detail about their¦101643 \add Jewish\add* law¦101642 and nothing¦101644 that should result in death¦101647 or imprisonment.
\v 30 Then¦101654 when I learnt that they have a plot¦101656 to try to ambush and kill him, I sent¦101668 him immediately¦101665 to you, and I'll order his accusers¦101674 to bring their case before¦101680 you.”
\p
\v 31 So¦101685 the soldiers¦101686 carried out their instructions and took¦101691 Paul¦101693 as far as Antipatris¦101700 during the night¦101697.
\v 32 The next¦101703 morning the foot soldiers returned¦101712 to the barracks¦101715, leaving the mounted soldiers to take him¦101710 on
\v 33 to Caesarea¦101720, where they¦101727 presented¦101727 Paul¦101730 to the governor¦101726 along with the letter¦101724.
\v 34 After reading the letter the governor asked¦101737 Paul what province¦101741 that he's from and¦101733 found out that he was from Cilicia¦101747.
\v 35 Then he ordered¦101763 Paul to be kept under guard¦101772 \add in the prison\add* in Herod's palace until he would hear¦101752 from him¦101773 in the future once his accusers¦101758 got there.
\c 24
\s1 The Jews accuse Paul before Governor Felix
\rem /s1 The Case against Paul; Paul before Felix at Caesarea; Paul Appears before Felix; The Trial Before Felix
\p
\v 1 Five¦101777 days¦101778 later, Ananias¦101782 the chief¦101781 priest¦101781 arrived along with some elders¦101785 and¦101787 an attorney¦101788 named Tertullos¦101789, to bring the charges against¦101795 Paul¦101797 before Governor¦101794 \add Felix\add*.
\v 2 Once he was called¦101798 to speak, Tertullos¦101804 began¦101801 his¦101800 accusation¦101802 of Paul:
\p “We have seen peace¦101807 under your rule and¦101799 the helpful reforms¦101812 that¦101817 this nation¦101816 has experienced through your provision¦101821,
\v 3 most excellent¦101827 Felix¦101828, and we¦101826 gratefully welcome¦101826 everything¦101822 you've done and everywhere¦101825 you've done it¦101826.
\v 4 But in order¦101832 not to oblige you longer than necessary, in your¦101846 gentleness¦101847 we request you to listen¦101841 to our brief¦101844 statement.
\v 5 This man¦101851 here has¦101848 been found¦101848 to be a troublemaker, stirring¦101856 up riots among the Jews throughout the inhabited¦101865 world¦101865, and as a ring-leader of the Nazarite sect¦101871
\v 6 he even attempted¦101876 to profane¦101877 the temple¦101875 where we¦101880 were able to arrest him.
\v 7 ◘
\v 8 When you examine¦101912 him yourself¦101911 you'll discover all these¦101915 things that¦101917 we're accusing¦101919 him of.”
\v 9 The Jews who had¦101930 come also affirmed all the accusations.
\s1 Paul's defence against the accusations
\rem /s1 Paul's Defense before Felix
\p
\v 10 So¦101932 the governor¦101939 nodded¦101936 for Paul¦101935 to speak¦101940 and¦101932 he started,
\p “I happily defend¦101956 myself¦101955 today knowing that¦101949 you¦101945 have¦101936 many years¦101943 of experience ruling \add in this region\add*.
\v 11 You can quickly establish that it was less than twelve¦101968 days¦101966 ago that I arrived in Yerushalem¦101975 to worship there¦101964.
\v 12 They never found¦101982 me in the temple¦101981 arguing with anyone¦101985 or stirring up the crowd¦101991, and¦101977 nor was I doing that in the \add ≈Jewish¦101995 meeting¦101995 halls¦101995\add* or anywhere in the city¦101999
\v 13 and so they can't provide evidence to you¦102005 to support any of their accusations¦102011.
\v 14 However, I must confess¦102013 that I follow ‘The Path’ that they¦102022 call¦102022 a sect¦102023 which¦102021 serves¦102025 the \nd God¦102028\nd* of our ancestors¦102027 and¦102035 believes¦102029 everything in \add Mosheh's\add* law¦102034 as well as the writings of the prophets¦102039.
\v 15 We put our trust in the same God that¦102047 they themselves¦102049 follow, and we all anticipate a resurrection¦102052 of those who serve God and those who don't.
\v 16 Because of this, I also strive to maintain a clean conscience¦102067 in everything¦102077, before \nd God¦102072\nd* and¦102073 the people¦102075.
\p
\v 17 After several years¦102079 away, I arrived¦102089 \add in Yerushalem\add* bringing offerings¦102091 to help my¦102088 own people and¦102090 before God.
\v 18 That was when they¦102095 found¦102095 me in the temple¦102100 after going through the purification¦102097 ceremony, and without either a crowd¦102103 or a commotion¦102106 around me.
\v 19 But¦102120 there were some Jews from \add the province of\add* Asia¦102112 \add Minor\add* who are the ones who should be appearing here before¦102117 you¦102118, and¦102120 they should be the ones accusing¦102121 me if they could find anything¦102123 against¦102125 me.
\v 20 Even these¦102130 ones here now should¦102131 explain what their council¦102142 found¦102134 me guilty of,
\v 21 other than my calling out that¦102148 I was being¦102160 judged¦102160 because I believe in the resurrection¦102157 of the dead¦102158.
\p
\v 22 But Felix¦102171 who was already familiar with followers of ‘The Path’, announced that the trial would be adjourned until Commander¦102184 Lysias¦102182 arrived when he would investigate¦102186 further.
\v 23 He ordered the centurion¦102194 to keep¦102195 Paul confined, but¦102200 under relaxed¦102201 conditions and allowing any of his¦102207 friends to bring him anything he needed.
\s1 Paul speaks to the governor and his wife
\rem /s1 Paul Held in Custody; Paul before Felix and Drusilla
\p
\v 24 However, several days¦102214 later Felix¦102219 returned there, \add this time\add* with his wife¦102224 Drusilla¦102221 who was Jewish. He sent¦102229 for Paul¦102231 and¦102232 listened to him¦102234 explain about faith¦102241 in the \nd messiah¦102238\nd*.
\v 25 But¦102248 when the subject turned to righteousness¦102246 and self-control¦102249 and the coming¦102255 judgement¦102253, Felix¦102262 became¦102260 very uncomfortable and told him¦102244, “That's enough for now. If I have time in the future, I'll¦102272 send for you¦102273.”
\v 26 However he was also hoping¦102277 that Paul¦102284 would give him money¦102279, so he often sent¦102292 for him and spoke with him.
\p
\v 27 This went on for two¦102296 years¦102296, until Felix was replaced as governor by Porcius¦102303 Festus¦102304, but¦102297 wanting¦102305 to stay in the good books of the Jews, Felix left¦102314 Paul¦102317 confined.
\c 25
\s1 Tried yet again, Paul appeals to Caesar
\rem /s1 The Trial Before Festus; Paul Appeals to the Emperor; Paul Appears before Festus
\p
\v 1 After Festus¦102319 had¦102321 been in the area for three¦102326 days¦102327, he made the uphill trip from Caesarea¦102332 to Yerushalem¦102330.
\v 2 There the chief¦102340 priests¦102340 and the Jewish leaders¦102343 repeated their case against¦102346 Paul¦102348, and they¦102350 implored¦102350 Festus,
\v 3 asking as a special favour¦102353, that he would order Paul to be sent¦102358 to Yerushalem¦102362 (because they wanted to ambush¦102363 and kill¦102365 him on the way¦102369).
\v 4 But¦102383 Festus¦102373 replied that¦102370 he'd keep¦102375 Paul¦102377 in Caesarea¦102380 and¦102383 he was heading back there soon,
\v 5 so he told them, “Some of your leaders can go there with me, and if the man¦102404 has¦102397 done anything¦102399 wrong, they can explain their accusations¦102407 there.”
\p
\v 6 After staying¦102409 seven or eight¦102417 days¦102413 in Yerushalem, Festus and company travelled back to Caesarea¦102422, and the next¦102425 day¦102424 he sat¦102426 at the judge's bench and ordered¦102430 that Paul¦102432 be brought¦102434 in.
\v 7 When¦102436 he arrived¦102435, the Jews who'd come¦102443 from Yerushalem¦102442 stood¦102438 facing Paul and¦102446 presented many heavy¦102447 charges¦102448 which¦102454 they¦102456 weren't able¦102456 to prove.
\v 8 Paul¦102460 defended¦102461 himself, stating that he hadn't sinned¦102478 against any Jewish law or against the temple¦102473, nor against any Roman law.
\p
\v 9 However, Festus¦102480 wanted¦102483 to be able to grant¦102488 a favour¦102487 to the Jews, so in response to Paul¦102491 he asked, “Are you willing¦102493 to be returned to Yerushalem¦102495 so that I can judge¦102501 you about these¦102499 things there¦102497?”
\p
\v 10 “I'm here facing Caesar's representative,” Paul¦102507 replied. “and it's quite appropriate¦102517 for me to be judged¦102518 here. I've¦102522 done¦102522 nothing¦102520 wrong¦102522 to these Jews as you¦102525 can no doubt see for yourself.
\v 11 If I had¦102537 done¦102537 anything wrong¦102533, anything worthy¦102535 of the death sentence, then I'm ready to accept death. On the other¦102544 hand, if none of their accusations¦102549 are true¦102546, no one can hand me over to them. I appeal to Caesar¦102557.”
\p
\v 12 So Festus¦102561 conferred with his legal counsel¦102566 and answered¦102567, “Well, you've appealed¦102569 to Caesar so you'll go¦102572 to Caesar!”
\s1 King Agrippa wants to hear from Paul
\rem /s1 Festus Consults King Agrippa; Paul before Agrippa and Bernice
\p
\v 13 Several days¦102573 later, King¦102579 Agrippa¦102577 and¦102580 \add his sister\add* Bernice¦102581 arrived¦102582 in Caesarea¦102585 to meet Festus¦102589.
\v 14 As they¦102594 \add had planned to\add* stay¦102594 on for a few days¦102593, Festus¦102598 mentioned about Paul¦102605 to the king¦102600, telling him, “There's¦102609 a prisoner¦102613 here that Felix¦102612 left¦102610 behind.
\v 15 When I was in Yerushalem¦102619, the chief¦102623 priests¦102623 and¦102624 Jewish elders¦102626 reported¦102620 to me, wanting a conviction¦102632 of this man.
\v 16 I told them that it's not the Roman way to convict someone until they've been able to face¦102654 their accusers¦102657 and¦102660 make their defence¦102661 against the accusations.
\v 17 So¦102667 as soon as they arrived here, on the very next¦102676 day¦102675 I sat¦102677 on the judge's bench and ordered¦102681 the man¦102684 to be brought¦102682 in
\v 18 that they were accusing¦102689, but they didn't¦102690 raise even one of the charges¦102691 that I expected.
\v 19 Instead they¦102708 raised some issues about their¦102705 own beliefs and¦102712 about some dead person called \nd Yeshua\nd* that Paul¦102720 reckoned was still living¦102721.
\v 20 I was puzzled about this debate¦102730 and¦102723 asked¦102731 him if he wanted to be judged¦102740 about these things in Yerushalem¦102738.
\v 21 But Paul¦102745 appealed¦102746 to be kept safe from them until he could¦102760 face the emperor, so I ordered¦102755 him to be kept \add in prison\add* until I can send¦102760 him to Rome.”
\p
\v 22 “I'd quite like to hear him myself¦102773,” Agrippa¦102765 said¦102780.
\rem /s1 Paul Before Agrippa; Paul Speaks to Agrippa; Paul Brought before Agrippa
\p “Well, tomorrow,” replied Festus¦102769, “you'll be able to hear from him.”
\p
\v 23 So¦102784 the next¦102785 day¦102783, \add King\add* Agrippa¦102788 and Bernice¦102791 formally arrived with all their attendants and entered the auditorium¦102800 to greet the commanders¦102804 and the prominent¦102809 men¦102806 of the city¦102812, and then Festus¦102816 ordered¦102814 for Paul¦102819 to be brought¦102817 in.
\v 24 Festus¦102823 started, “King¦102825 Agrippa¦102824 and everyone present¦102829, observe¦102832 the prisoner who a multitude¦102839 of Jews pleaded¦102842 with me about both in Yerushalem¦102847 and here, that¦102833 he doesn't deserve to still be alive.
\v 25 But¦102870 I haven't discovered anything that he's done¦102867 that's worthy¦102863 of a death¦102865 sentence, and¦102870 since he himself¦102869 appealed¦102874 to the emperor, I judged¦102877 that that's where he should be sent¦102878.
\v 26 The problem is that I don't¦102888 have any charge to write to my master¦102887 and¦102896 so I've brought¦102892 the prisoner out in front of you so that after you examine¦102904 him¦102893, I might have something¦102908 I can write,
\v 27 because¦102912 it¦102914 doesn't seem logical to me to transport a prisoner¦102916 \add to Rome\add* if there's no charges¦102922 specified against¦102920 him¦102921.”
\c 26
\s1 Paul's defence before Festus and Agrippa
\rem /s1 Paul Defends Himself before Agrippa
\p
\v 1 So then Agrippa¦102924 told Paul¦102928, “Okay, you¦102931 can give your side now then.”
\m So Paul gestured with his hand¦102942 and¦102925 began:
\p
\v 2 “Concerning¦102944 all the charges which¦102946 have¦102952 been brought against me by the Jews, King¦102950 Agrippa¦102951, I consider¦102952 myself¦102953 fortunate
\v 3 because you're an expert on Jewish customs¦102976 and¦102978 issues, so I ask you to listen¦102985 to me patiently¦102984.
\p
\v 4 “All the Jews know¦103006 about my early life, from my upbringing in my own country¦103001 and¦103004 then in Yerushalem¦103005.
\v 5 They also know¦103010, and could¦103014 even testify¦103015 to you if they¦103014 would admit it, that I followed our¦103022 religious¦103023 laws very closely as a member¦103025 of the Pharisees¦103025 party¦103025.
\v 6 Yet now I stand¦103041 judged¦103042 for believing in the same hope as our ancestors¦103034—that hope promised¦103036 by our \nd God¦103040\nd*
\v 7 who we twelve¦103046 tribes¦103046 diligently serve¦103053 day¦103052 and¦103051 night¦103050 in order to obtain—the hope¦103060, \add ≈your majesty\add*, for which¦103044 I'm being¦103061 indicted¦103061.
\v 8 So why do you¦103072 all¦103072 find it¦103070 unbelievable¦103069 that \nd God¦103075\nd* can raise¦103077 the dead¦103076?
\p
\v 9 “I too \add originally\add* thought it¦103090 necessary¦103090 to do¦103093 many things to oppose the cause of this \nd Yeshua\nd* from \nd Nazareth¦103089\nd*.
\v 10 So in Yerushalem¦103100 I worked to have¦103115 many of these innocent believers locked¦103109 up in prison¦103108 with the authority¦103114 of the chief¦103113 priests¦103113, or if they were to be killed¦103116, I would add my vote¦103121 against¦103119 them.
\v 11 And in all the \add ≈Jewish¦103126 meeting¦103126 halls¦103126\add*, \add when I found believers\add* I punished¦103128 them and forced them to curse \add God\add* and treated them with extreme anger, even travelling to further away towns.
\s1 Paul tells about his commission
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Conversion
\rem /r (Acts 9:1-19; 22:6-16)
\p
\v 12 “So it was one time travelling to Damascus¦103149 carrying permission¦103153 letters from the chief¦103157 priests¦103157
\v 13 that in the middle¦103159 of the day¦103158, \add ≈your majesty\add*, I saw¦103163 a bright light¦103173 from the sky—even brighter than the sun¦103170—and it shone¦103171 on me and my companions.
\v 14 All of us fell¦103182 down to the ground¦103186 and¦103180 I heard¦103187 a voice¦103188 speaking to me in Hebrew¦103196 saying¦103190, ‘Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting¦103202 me? Life will be difficult for you if you resist me.’
\p
\v 15 ‘Who are you, master?’, I asked.
\p “And the master responded¦103210, ‘I am \nd Yeshua\nd*, the one you're¦103212 persecuting¦103223.
\v 16 Now get up, because¦103234 I let you see me in order to appoint¦103237 you as my servant and my witness¦103242—to tell the people what you've seen and what you will be shown.
\v 17 I will rescue¦103251 you from your own people¦103255 and¦103256 from the non-Jews that I'm sending¦103265 you out to,
\v 18 to open¦103268 their minds and for them to turn¦103274 back from darkness¦103276 to light¦103278 and from Satan's¦103284 power¦103282 to \nd God¦103287\nd*, so that¦103299 they can be forgiven¦103291 for their sins¦103292 and receive¦103289 an inheritance among¦103295 the ones who've been made guiltless by their faith¦103298 in me.’
\s1 Paul tells about his ministry
\rem /s1 Paul Tells of His Work; Paul Tells of His Preaching
\p
\v 19 “So then, King¦103303 Agrippa¦103304, I couldn't disobey that vision¦103310
\v 20 so I preached first¦103315 to those in Damascus¦103314 and then in Yerushalem¦103319 and all of Yudea¦103327, and then in the towns of the non-Jews. I preached about turning from their sins and turning back to \nd God¦103339\nd*, and then actually living like people who had repented¦103334.
\v 21 It was because of these¦103346 things that the Jews grabbed me in the temple¦103356 and tried to do me in.
\v 22 So¦103360 it is that with God's help¦103359 until¦103367 this very day¦103369 I have¦103371 stood¦103371 testifying¦103372 to commoners and leaders, teaching nothing¦103378 other than what Mosheh¦103389 and the prophets¦103384 had already written that would happen¦103387—
\v 23 that the \nd messiah¦103393\nd* would \add come and\add* suffer¦103391 and as the first¦103395 of the dead¦103398 to come back to life, he is going¦103400 to be proclaiming¦103402 light¦103399 to both Jews and non-Jews.”
\rem /s1 Paul Appeals to Agrippa to Believe
\p
\v 24 Then¦103410 speaking about Paul's defence¦103412, Governor Festus¦103414 said¦103419, “Paul, you're stark, raving¦103420 mad¦103420! All your education has turned¦103429 you into an idiot.”
\p
\v 25 But¦103440 Paul¦103432 said¦103435, “I haven't gone mad¦103434, most excellent¦103436 Festus¦103437, but¦103438 I'm speaking¦103443 the truth¦103439 in all seriousness.
\v 26 The king¦103449 knows what I'm talking¦103454 about and that's why I'm speaking boldly¦103453, because¦103445 none of these things were done¦103468 in a corner¦103467 and I'm sure that none of it has¦103468 escaped¦103455 his notice¦103455.
\v 27 King¦103471 Agrippa¦103473, do you believe what the prophets¦103475 \add wrote\add*? Yes, I'm sure you do.”
\p
\v 28 But¦103480 Agrippa¦103481 answered Paul¦103484, “Do you think you can talk me into becoming a follower¦103491 of the messiah¦103491 in just that short time¦103487?”
\p
\v 29 “I hope¦103498 to \nd God¦103502\nd*”, said¦103496 Paul¦103496, “whether it takes a short time or a long time, that both you¦103512 and¦103495 the others listening today¦103519 will become¦103520 just like me except¦103527 for these¦103530 chains.”
\p
\v 30 Then the king¦103538 and the governor¦103541 both stood, along with Bernice¦103544 and the others sitting¦103547 with them
\v 31 and¦103549 as they left they were saying¦103554 to each other, “That man¦103566 hasn't done¦103564 anything deserving of death¦103558 or imprisonment.”
\p
\v 32 “Yes, he could have¦103573 been set free,” replied Agrippa¦103568, “if he hadn't appealed¦103580 to Caesar¦103581.”
\c 27
\s1 Paul is escorted off overseas
\rem /s1 Paul Sails for Rome; Paul's journey to Rome
\p
\v 1 So¦103583 when it¦103584 was considered the right season to sail¦103586 to Italy¦103590, Paul¦103595 and¦103596 some other¦103598 prisoners¦103599 were put under the guard of a centurion¦103600 named Julius (from the Augustan¦103605 Regiment).
\v 2 We all boarded¦103606 on a ship¦103608 from Adramyttium¦103609 that was about to sail¦103612 to various places¦103618 along¦103615 the coast of Asia¦103617 \add Minor\add*. Aristarchus¦103623, a Macedonian¦103625 man from Thessalonica¦103626, was with us as we¦103619 departed.
\p
\v 3 The next¦103629 day¦103629 we¦103630 docked at Tsidon where Julius very kindly allowed Paul¦103638 to visit some of his friends¦103643 so they could provide for his needs.
\v 4 When¦103648 we¦103650 left again, the ship stayed between Cyprus¦103652 and¦103648 the mainland because¦103653 of the unfavourable winds¦103656,
\v 5 then¦103660 after sailing¦103668 past Cilicia¦103665 and¦103666 Pamfylia¦103667, we¦103669 docked at Myra¦103673 in \add the province\add* of Lycia¦103675.
\v 6 There the centurion¦103680 found¦103678 a ship¦103681 from Alexandria¦103682 that was sailing¦103683 to Italy¦103686, so he boarded us on that.
\p
\v 7 After many days¦103695 of slow¦103696 and difficult¦103698 sailing¦103696, we came alongside Cnidus¦103702 but¦103694 the wind¦103707 wasn't helpful to us, so we sailed¦103708 across to Crete¦103710 and along the cape of Salmone.
\v 8 We sailed¦103715 along¦103715 it¦103716 with difficulty¦103713 until we¦103717 came¦103717 to a place¦103719 called¦103721 ‘Fair Havens’ which¦103724 was near the city¦103727 of Lasea.
\p
\v 9 A lot of time¦103732 had now elapsed¦103733 and¦103734 the voyage¦103739 was starting to become dangerous¦103737 because¦103740 we were already heading into winter. Paul¦103749 advised¦103747 those in charge,
\v 10 telling them, “Men, I perceive¦103753 that we're going to encounter a lot of damage¦103756 on this voyage¦103777, with loss¦103759 not only of the cargo¦103765 and the ship¦103768, but¦103757 also our¦103773 lives¦103772.”
\v 11 But¦103783 the centurion¦103780 was persuaded¦103788 by the ship¦103785 owner¦103785 and¦103783 the captain¦103782, rather¦103787 than by what Paul¦103793 had said.
\v 12 They realised that this harbour wasn't suitable to winter in, so the majority suggested leaving there¦103807 with the expectation of being¦103799 able¦103811 to get to Crete¦103819 to winter in the harbour at Phoenix¦103815 (which was open to both the southwest¦103822 and¦103823 the northwest¦103825).
\s1 The severe storm at sea
\rem /s1 The Storm at Sea
\p
\v 13 When¦103828 a south¦103829 wind¦103829 started blowing¦103826 gently¦103826, they thought they could make it, so they pulled in the anchor and¦103828 sailed¦103836 along¦103836 close to Crete¦103838.
\v 14 However¦103851, a short while later the wind¦103847 turned to a violent north-easter which¦103849 forced us out to sea.
\v 15 The ship¦103855 was driven by the wind¦103861 and unable to tack to face into it, so the sailors gave¦103862 up and just let it be driven.
\v 16 As we passed a small island¦103864 called¦103868 Clauda¦103869, we got a brief break and¦103865 were able¦103872 to resecure the lifeboat¦103877
\v 17 and then¦103887 bring it up on board. Then¦103887 ropes were used to wrap around the ship in case it were to run¦103892 aground¦103892 into the shallows¦103890 at Syrtis¦103891 and the gear¦103895 was lowered¦103893,\f + \fr 27:17 \ft It's not certain what was meant here—it might have meant lowering the sails further to catch less wind or even lowering the mast, or else letting out the sea anchor to help keep the back of the ship facing the wind.\f* allowing the wind to drive¦103897 the ship along¦103897.
\v 18 We were violently¦103898 tossed¦103901 around by the storm¦103901, and¦103900 the next¦103904 day¦103903 the crew started \add lightening the ship by\add* tipping \add some of the cargo\add* overboard.
\v 19 By the third¦103909 day¦103909, the sailors themselves \add in desperation\add* were even throwing¦103916 some of the ship's¦103914 gear overboard.
\v 20 We didn't see either the sun¦103919 or the stars¦103921 for several more days¦103925—it wasn't a minor storm¦103926 by any means—and¦103927 by then¦103918 any remaining hope¦103934 that¦103936 we had \add of staying alive\add* disappeared
\v 21 and no one had eaten or drunk much at all.
\p Then¦103940 Paul¦103947 stood¦103945 up in the middle of them and said¦103951, “Well, you should have listened to me after all and not left Crete¦103962, so now we have this damage¦103966 and loss¦103970.
\v 22 Nevertheless I think we can all cheer¦103976 up because¦103978 not a single life¦103979 will be lost—only the ship¦103987—
\v 23 because¦103989 last night¦103994 one of the messengers¦104005 of the \nd God¦103998\nd* that¦103992 I serve¦104004 came and stood¦103988 beside me
\v 24 and told me not to be afraid¦104008 because I would indeed get to stand¦104013 before Caesar¦104010 and that \nd God¦104019\nd* has¦104016 granted¦104016 your lives to me as well.
\v 25 So¦104025 cheer¦104026 up, fellows, because¦104029 I'm certain that \nd God¦104031\nd* will carry out what was told to me
\v 26 even though we'll run¦104048 aground¦104048 onto an island¦104042 first.”
\rem /s1 The Shipwreck
\p
\v 27 So it happened that in the middle¦104061 of our fourteenth¦104051 night of being¦104055 blown across the Adriatic¦104059 Sea, the sailors¦104066 suspected¦104064 that we were nearing land¦104072.
\v 28 They dropped a weighted line and measured the ocean depth at around 40 metres and then¦104074 a bit later it was down to 30 metres.
\v 29 Worrying that the ship might run¦104102 aground¦104102 on rough¦104099 \add rocks\add*, they threw out four¦104108 anchors¦104107 from the stern¦104105 as they hoped that daylight would come quickly.
\v 30 Then¦104113 the sailors¦104114 lowered¦104122 the lifeboat¦104124 as if planning to pull out some anchors¦104133 from the bow¦104131, but¦104113 really planning to desert the ship¦104120.
\v 31 However Paul¦104138 told the centurion¦104140 and¦104141 the soldiers¦104143 that if the sailors didn't¦104145 remain¦104147 with the ship¦104150, everybody couldn't be sure to be saved¦104153,
\v 32 so the soldiers¦104159 cut¦104157 the ropes¦104162 attaching the lifeboat¦104164 and¦104165 allowed¦104166 it to run¦104168 aground¦104168.
\p
\v 33 As they waited for the daylight, Paul¦104179 encouraged them all to eat something, saying¦104183, “Today¦104185 is our fourteenth¦104184 day and¦104170 you've had¦104191 nothing¦104190 to eat. As we're waiting¦104187,
\v 34 I urge you to eat something. This will help you to have strength because none of you are going to drown.”
\v 35 Having said¦104220 that, he gave¦104226 thanks¦104226 to \nd God¦104229\nd* in front of everyone, and¦104221 breaking¦104233 some bread¦104225, started¦104234 eating¦104235 it¦104233.
\v 36 They all cheered¦104236 up and¦104237 also started eating.
\v 37 There were two¦104260 hundred¦104260 and¦104249 seventy-six¦104261 of them on the ship¦104258,
\v 38 and¦104265 when they'd all eaten what they wanted, they lightened¦104268 the ship¦104270 more by throwing¦104271 their wheat¦104273 into the sea¦104276.
\s1 The ship runs aground
\rem /s1 The Shipwreck
\p
\v 39 Once it became¦104280 light, they saw that¦104294 they were close to shore¦104291 in a bay¦104286 but¦104278 didn't¦104283 recognise¦104285 the place. After some discussion, they decided to try to save the ship¦104304
\v 40 so they cut¦104309 away the anchors¦104307 that were holding them in the sea¦104313 and¦104305 untied the rudder¦104319 \add handles\add*. Then¦104305 raising the foresail¦104323, they let the wind¦104324 blow¦104325 them into the shore¦104329.
\v 41 But¦104338 they¦104335 hit a bank with ocean on both¦104334 sides of it and the ship ran¦104335 aground¦104335. The bow¦104342 of the ship was completely stuck¦104343 but¦104331 the stern¦104349 was being¦104350 broken up by the force¦104354 of the ocean.
\p
\v 42 At this point the soldiers¦104359 decided that they¦104365 should kill¦104365 the prisoners¦104364 in case any of them decided to swim¦104368 away and¦104358 escape¦104369,
\v 43 but¦104372 the centurion¦104373 wanted to keep Paul¦104377 safe so he vetoed that plan. He commanded¦104384 those who could swim to jump off and swim in to shore,
\v 44 and for the others¦104407 to grab \add floating\add* planks¦104405 or something off the ship¦104413 \add that would float\add*, and so everyone¦104403 got safely¦104418 onto the beach.
\c 28
\s1 On the island of Malta
\rem /s1 In Malta; Ashore on Malta; Paul on the Island of Malta
\p
\v 1 Once everyone was safe, we¦104426 discovered that the island¦104430 is called¦104431 Malta.
\v 2 And the people there showed incredible kindness¦104440 to us as they¦104447 welcomed us and lit¦104442 a fire¦104445, as it started raining again and was very cold¦104458.
\v 3 However when Paul¦104462 was helping to pick up sticks¦104463 and putting them on the fire¦104473, a viper¦104474 was driven out by the heat¦104478 and clamped¦104481 its jaws down on his¦104485 hand¦104484.
\v 4 When¦104487 the islanders saw¦104488 the snake hanging from his hand¦104496 they¦104501 decided that¦104507 he must be a murderer¦104503 who had survived the shipwreck but¦104487 now fate had finally decided his time was up.
\v 5 However, Paul just shook¦104521 the snake off into the fire¦104527 and wasn't harmed.
\v 6 The locals were waiting¦104547 for his arm to swell¦104538 up or for him to fall¦104540 down dead¦104542, but¦104532 after a while when nothing¦104550 happened they changed¦104555 their¦104555 minds¦104555 and¦104548 decided that instead, he must be a god¦104562.
\p
\v 7 The area that we had¦104579 beached in belonged to the leader¦104573 of the island¦104575 named Publius¦104577. He welcomed us and¦104564 looked after us for three¦104581 days¦104582,
\v 8 but¦104587 while we were there, his father¦104589 got sick with dysentery¦104594 and fever¦104592. Paul¦104601 went in and placing his hands¦104608 on him, prayed¦104604 for him and healed¦104610 him.
\v 9 As a result of that¦104619, anyone on the whole island¦104624 who was sick came and¦104613 was cured
\v 10 and so the people brought us many gifts and when we left, they supplied us with provisions.
\s1 Sailing from Malta to Rome
\rem /s1 Arrival at Rome; From Malta to Rome; Paul Arrives at Rome
\p
\v 11 After three¦104649 months¦104650 we¦104652 were able to leave on an Alexandrian¦104659 ship¦104654 that had¦104655 wintered¦104655 at the island¦104658. It was decorated with carvings of the twin ‘gods’.
\v 12 We stayed three¦104669 days¦104667 when we¦104666 arrived at Syracuse¦104665
\v 13 and¦104677 then¦104677 we sailed on to Rhegium¦104676. The next day a south¦104682 wind¦104682 came¦104684 so we sailed on to Puteoli¦104686
\v 14 where¦104687 we found¦104688 some believers¦104689 and¦104698 they encouraged us to stay for seven¦104697 days¦104696. Then¦104698 we \add finally\add* got to Rome¦104703.
\v 15 The believers¦104707 in Rome had heard¦104708 about us, so they came¦104712 to meet¦104715 us at the Appius¦104720 Market¦104721 and the Three¦104723 Taverns¦104724. Paul¦104728 was encouraged to see¦104726 them and thanked¦104729 \nd God¦104731\nd*.
\s1 Paul preaches in Rome
\rem /s1 In Rome
\p
\v 16 When¦104735 we¦104736 got into Rome¦104740 proper, Paul¦104751 was allowed to live by himself¦104756, with just a soldier¦104761 guarding¦104759 him¦104760.
\rem /s1 Paul Preaches at Rome under Guard; Paul and Jewish Leaders in Rome
\p
\v 17 After three¦104766 days¦104765, he called¦104767 together the leaders¦104775 of the Jews \add in Rome\add*, and when they'd all gathered he spoke to them, “Men, brothers¦104786, I have done¦104790 nothing¦104788 against my people¦104792 or against our ancestral¦104797 customs¦104795. I was taken prisoner¦104798 in Yerushalem¦104800 and handed over to the Romans
\v 18 who, after examining¦104808 me, wanted to release me because¦104814 I had¦104808 done nothing worthy of execution.
\v 19 But in defending myself against the Jews there, I felt compelled¦104826 to appeal¦104827 to Caesar¦104828, although I myself had¦104835 no complaint against the Jews.
\v 20 So¦104841 that¦104840 was why I called for you and¦104848 wanted to address¦104849 you, because¦104851 I am in chains¦104857 for the sake of Yisrael's hope—\add the messiah\add*.”
\p
\v 21 “We haven't received¦104872 any correspondence here about you,” they¦104860 answered. “Nor have¦104877 any of our brothers¦104880 \add from Yerushalem\add* arrived¦104877 here to report¦104881 any crimes of yours.
\v 22 But we would very much like to hear¦104893 from you about your thinking¦104895, because we've certainly heard about this new sect¦104900 because everyone¦104907 is speaking¦104908 out against¦104908 it.”
\rem /s1 Paul Preaches in Rome
\p
\v 23 Then¦104910 they set a day¦104912 when they could visit him where he was staying and so that from morning¦104948 to evening¦104950 he could explain¦104922 about God's kingdom¦104928 and show them how Mosheh's law¦104942 and the prophets' writings spoke about \nd Yeshua\nd*.
\v 24 Afterwards some were persuaded¦104955 while others¦104959 didn't find it believable.
\v 25 They argued back and forth, and as they¦104967 were about to leave, Paul¦104970 shared this final¦104972 message¦104971, “The holy¦104978 \nd spirit¦104976\nd* spoke¦104979 rightly through Yeshayah¦104982 the prophet¦104984 to your¦104988 ancestors¦104987, saying:
\q1
\v 26 Go to these people¦104999 and tell¦104994 them,
\q1 When you listen you will hear¦105003 but not understand¦105009,
\q1 and when you look you will see but not perceive¦105017 it.
\q1
\v 27 Because¦105020 these people's¦105024 minds have become¦105019 dull¦105019
\q2 and they can barely hear with their ears¦105028
\q2 and they shut¦105036 their eyes¦105034,
\q1 just in case their eyes might actually see¦105038,
\q2 and their ears might actually hear,
\q2 and they might understand¦105048 with their minds,
\q1 and turn¦105050 back to me
\q2 and then I would heal¦105053 them.
\p
\v 28 So¦105057 now let it¦105058 be known¦105056 to you¦105059 all¦105059 that¦105065 the \add message of\add* salvation¦105067 from \nd God¦105069\nd* has been sent¦105064 out to non-Jews and¦105071 they¦105070 will listen.”
\p
\v 29 ◘
\v 30 Paul remained¦105085 in his own rental¦105095 accommodation in Rome for two¦105091 whole¦105092 years¦105091 and¦105096 welcomed¦105097 everyone who went to see him¦105102,
\v 31 telling them about God's kingdom¦105105 and¦105108 boldly teaching¦105109 about the \nd master¦105113 Yeshua\nd* without any hindrance.
